# The Gap: A Neural Network Is Just a Function
@mastery L2
@prereq the-systems-lens algorithmic-vs-systems-optimization-choosing-the-right-lever

If you have built a neural network from scratch, you know what it really is: a function. Input vector, matrix multiplications, non-linearities, output vector. That is it. A 175-billion-parameter GPT model is the same thing — just bigger. The parameters are knobs, and the network is a very expensive knob-turning machine.

The gap between that function and ChatGPT is not inside the network. It is everything wrapped around it. The neural network never sees text. It never "chooses" words. It never "remembers" your previous message. All of that is infrastructure bolted on by engineers.

Here is what actually lives outside the neural network:

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A["Raw Neural Network<br>f(x) = y"] -->|"wrap in"| B["Tokenizer"]
    B --> C["Positional Encoding"]
    C --> D["Causal Attention Mask"]
    D --> E["Core Transformer"]
    E --> F["Output Logits"]
    F --> G["Softmax + Sampling"]
    G --> H["Working LLM"]
```

The network itself is only the box labeled "Core Transformer." Everything else is scaffolding. If you think the model "understands" English, you are wrong. It understands integers between 0 and 50,000. The tokenizer translates your English into those integers before the network ever runs. The sampler translates the network's output integers back into English after it finishes.

::: callout info
A 175B parameter GPT-3 model is just a function with 175 billion knobs. The magic is in what you feed it and what you do with the output.
:::

::: quiz mcq L2
What does the tokenizer do BEFORE the neural network sees the input?
- [ ] It trains the model weights
- [x] It converts text into integer token IDs
- [ ] It runs the forward pass
- [ ] It applies the softmax function
explain: Tokenization is a pre-processing step. The neural network only sees numbers, never raw text.
:::

::: grounding
includes: The conceptual gap between a raw neural network function and a production LLM system
know: A neural network is a mathematical function mapping input vectors to output vectors [goodfellow2016]
know: An LLM requires tokenization, positional encoding, attention masking, and sampling infrastructure external to the core network [vaswani2017]
source: goodfellow2016 | Deep Learning | https://www.deeplearningbook.org/
source: vaswani2017 | Attention Is All You Need | https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762
:::

# Tokenization: The Input Bridge
@mastery L2

Before the neural network runs, your text must become numbers. This happens in the tokenizer — a completely separate program from the neural network. The tokenizer has its own vocabulary, typically 32,000 to 100,000 tokens, fixed at training time. It never changes during inference.

Byte Pair Encoding (BPE) is the standard algorithm. It starts with characters and iteratively merges the most frequent pairs into new tokens. "Transformer" might become ["Transform", "er"]. The model never sees the word "Transformer" as a single unit unless that exact sequence was merged into the vocabulary during training.

Here is a simplified but complete tokenizer in pure Python:

```python
# Simplified BPE-style tokenization
vocab = {
    "the": 0, "cat": 1, "sat": 2, "on": 3,
    "mat": 4, "dog": 5, "ran": 6, "a": 7
}

def encode(text):
    words = text.lower().split()
    return [vocab[w] for w in words if w in vocab]

def decode(token_ids):
    reverse = {v: k for k, v in vocab.items()}
    return " ".join([reverse[i] for i in token_ids])

text = "the cat sat on the mat"
tokens = encode(text)
print(f"Text:    '{text}'")
print(f"Tokens:  {tokens}")
print(f"Decoded: '{decode(tokens)}'")
```

In reality, tokenizers use subword units, not whole words. "unhappiness" might tokenize to ["un", "happiness"] or ["un", "happ", "iness"]. The neural network receives only the integer IDs. It has no idea what letters those IDs represent.

