# Inside the Stack: One Network or Many?
@mastery L3

A common confusion: is a Transformer multiple neural networks working together? No. It is one very deep neural network. The "layers" you hear about are not separate networks. They are repeated applications of the same two operations — self-attention and a feed-forward network — with residual connections and layer normalization between them.

Think of it as a for-loop, not a pipeline of independent models:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Input["Token Embeddings<br>x_0"] --> L1["Layer 1<br>Self-Attn + FFN"]
    L1 --> L2["Layer 2<br>Self-Attn + FFN"]
    L2 --> L3["Layer 3<br>Self-Attn + FFN"]
    L3 -->|"..."| LL["Layer L<br>Self-Attn + FFN"]
    LL --> Output["Final Hidden State<br>h"]
```

Each layer updates the representation:

$$
x_{l+1} = \text{LayerNorm}(x_l + \text{Attention}(x_l) + \text{FFN}(x_l))
$$

The weights for every layer are stored inside the same model checkpoint. When you load "Llama-3-8B," you are loading one network with 32 layers, not 32 networks.

But here is the critical systems insight: some of what happens inside the forward pass is not "learning" at all. It is engineered machinery:

| Component | Type | Why It Matters |
|-----------|------|--------------|
| Weight matrices | Learned | Store knowledge from training data |
| Layer norm params | Learned | Stabilize training dynamics |
| Attention mask | Engineered | Prevents the model from looking at future tokens |
| Positional encoding | Engineered | Tells the model where each token sits in the sequence |
| Residual connections | Engineered | Enable gradient flow through deep stacks |

The mask and positional encoding are not learned. They are computed on the fly by the inference engine. This is part of the gap between "a neural network" and "an LLM system."

::: grounding
includes: The transformer architecture as a single deep network composed of stacked identical layers
know: A transformer consists of L identical layers each containing multi-head self-attention and a feed-forward network [vaswani2017]
know: Layer normalization and residual connections are architectural components that enable training but are not the core learned function [vaswani2017]
source: vaswani2017 | Attention Is All You Need | https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762
:::

# The Output Layer: How Neurons Become Tokens
@mastery L3

This is the question you actually care about. When the final layer produces its hidden state $h \in \mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{hidden}}}$, what happens next?

The output layer is a fixed linear projection. It is not dynamic. It does not adjust per request. It is a matrix $W \in \mathbb{R}^{V \times d_{\text{hidden}}}$ where $V$ is the vocabulary size — typically 32,000 to 100,000. Each row of $W$ corresponds to exactly one token in the fixed vocabulary.

The math is brutal in its simplicity:

$$
\text{logits} = h W^T + b
$$

$$
P(t_i) = \frac{e^{\text{logit}_i}}{\sum_{j=1}^{V} e^{\text{logit}_j}}
$$

Every token in the vocabulary gets one logit. Every logit gets one probability. The neural network does not "choose" the token. It produces a score for every possible token, and the inference engine samples from that distribution.

Here is a complete NumPy simulation:

```python
import numpy as np

vocab_size = 50257   # GPT-2 vocabulary
hidden_dim = 768

# Simulated final hidden state from the last layer
h = np.random.randn(hidden_dim)

# Output projection matrix (learned during training, frozen now)
W = np.random.randn(vocab_size, hidden_dim) * 0.02
b = np.zeros(vocab_size)

# One matrix multiplication produces every logit
logits = W @ h + b
print(f"Vocabulary size:     {vocab_size}")
print(f"Hidden state shape:  {h.shape}")
print(f"Output logits shape: {logits.shape}")
print(f"Each logit maps to exactly ONE token in the fixed vocabulary")

# Softmax
probs = np.exp(logits - np.max(logits))
probs = probs / np.sum(probs)

# Sampling strategies live OUTSIDE the network
token_id = np.random.choice(vocab_size, p=probs)
print(f"Sampled token ID:    {token_id}")
```

