# Parallelism: Multiple Networks and GPUs
@mastery L3

You asked how multiple neural networks run in parallel. Here is the truth: a standard GPT-style LLM is one network, but it is so large that no single GPU can hold it. We parallelize the same network across many GPUs. There are three strategies:

**Data parallelism:** Copy the entire model to each GPU. Each GPU processes a different batch of data. Gradients are averaged across GPUs. This is training.

**Tensor parallelism:** Split individual weight matrices across GPUs. For the output layer, GPU 0 might hold the first half of the vocabulary logits, GPU 1 the second half. They compute in parallel, then gather.

**Pipeline parallelism:** Assign consecutive layers to different GPUs. GPU 0 runs layers 1–10, GPU 1 runs layers 11–20, and so on.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    H["Hidden State<br>h ∈ R^768"] --> GPU0["GPU 0<br>W[:25000] @ h"]
    H --> GPU1["GPU 1<br>W[25000:] @ h"]
    GPU0 --> G["All-Gather"]
    GPU1 --> G
    G --> Logits["Full Logits<br>∈ R^50000"]
```

While a standard GPT is monolithic, some architectures do use multiple distinct networks:

- **Encoder-decoder:** Two separate transformers (e.g., T5, BART)
- **Speculative decoding:** A small draft model generates candidates; the large model verifies them in parallel
- **Multi-modal:** A vision encoder feeds into an LLM backbone

But the dominant paradigm — GPT-4, Claude, Llama — is one network, sliced across many GPUs.

::: quiz mcq L3
In tensor parallelism, how is a single layer's weight matrix distributed?
- [ ] Each GPU gets a copy of the full matrix
- [x] Each GPU gets a slice of the matrix (e.g., half the rows)
- [ ] Each GPU gets a different layer entirely
- [ ] The matrix is dynamically resized per request
explain: Tensor parallelism splits individual weight matrices across GPUs. For the output layer, each GPU might handle a subset of the vocabulary logits.
:::

::: grounding
includes: How transformer inference and training are parallelized across multiple GPUs through data, tensor, and pipeline parallelism
know: Data parallelism replicates the model across GPUs with different data batches [li2020]
know: Tensor parallelism splits individual layers across multiple GPUs [shoeybi2019]
know: Pipeline parallelism assigns consecutive layers to different GPUs [huang2019]
source: li2020 | PyTorch Distributed | https://pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/dist_overview.html
source: shoeybi2019 | Megatron-LM | https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.08053
source: huang2019 | GPipe | https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.06965
:::

# The Full Inference Machine
@mastery L4

Putting it all together, here is what happens when you send a prompt to ChatGPT. The neural network is only one step in a long pipeline:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Prompt["User Prompt"] --> Tok["Tokenizer<br>(external)"]
    Tok --> Emb["Embeddings + Positional Encoding<br>(external)"]
    Emb --> Trans["Transformer Stack<br>(the neural network)"]
    Trans --> Logits["Output Logits<br>(fixed projection)"]
    Logits --> Soft["Softmax<br>(external)"]
    Soft --> Sample["Sampling<br>temperature, top-p<br>(external)"]
    Sample --> Out{"Is EOS token?"}
    Out -->|"No"| Tok
    Out -->|"Yes"| Detok["Detokenizer<br>(external)"]
    Detok --> Resp["Response Text"]
```

Notice the loop. The model generates one token at a time. That token is fed back in as input for the next forward pass. This is autoregressive generation. The neural network has no memory of previous tokens inside its weights — it relies on the KV-cache, an external data structure that stores key and value tensors from previous steps so attention does not recompute them.

| Element | Source | Fixed or Dynamic? |
|---------|--------|-------------------|
| Weight matrices | Training | Fixed at inference |
| Tokenizer rules | BPE merge table | Fixed |
| Vocabulary | Training corpus | Fixed |
| Attention mask | Engineered per step | Dynamic per sequence |
| KV-cache | Accumulated during generation | Dynamic |
| Sampling parameters | User/config | Dynamic |

::: html
<style>
  body { font-family: system-ui; background: #1c1917; color: #fafaf9; padding: 16px; }
  canvas { border: 1px solid #44403c; border-radius: 8px; display: block; margin: 12px auto; }
  .controls { text-align: center; margin-top: 8px; }
  button { background: #44403c; color: #fafaf9; border: 1px solid #57534e; padding: 6px 16px; border-radius: 6px; cursor: pointer; margin: 0 4px; }
  button:hover { background: #57534e; }
  .status { font-size: 12px; color: #a8a29e; text-align: center; margin-top: 8px; }
</style>
<div>
  <canvas id="c" width="500" height="200"></canvas>
  <div class="controls">
    <button onclick="reset()">Reset</button>
    <button onclick="step()">Step</button>
    <button onclick="auto()">Auto</button>
  </div>
  <div class="status" id="status">x = 10.0, loss = 100.0</div>
</div>
<script>
  const ctx = document.getElementById('c').getContext('2d');
  let x = 10, lr = 0.1, history = [];
  function draw() {
    ctx.fillStyle = '#1c1917'; ctx.fillRect(0,0,500,200);
    ctx.strokeStyle = '#57534e'; ctx.beginPath();
    for (let px = 0; px < 500; px++) {
      let xv = (px - 250) / 20, py = 180 - xv*xv*1.5;
      px===0 ? ctx.moveTo(px,py) : ctx.lineTo(px,py);
    }
    ctx.stroke();
    ctx.fillStyle = '#f59e0b';
    history.forEach(([hx, hloss]) => {
      ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(250+hx*20, 180-hloss*1.5, 3, 0, Math.PI*2); ctx.fill();
    });
    ctx.fillStyle = '#ef4444';
    ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(250+x*20, 180-x*x*1.5, 5, 0, Math.PI*2); ctx.fill();
    document.getElementById('status').textContent = `x = ${x.toFixed(3)}, loss = ${(x*x).toFixed(3)}`;
  }
  function step() { history.push([x, x*x]); x -= lr * 2*x; draw(); parent.postMessage({type:'widget:height', height: 280}, '*'); }
  function reset() { x = 10; history = []; draw(); }
  function auto() { let i = setInterval(() => { if (Math.abs(x) < 0.01) clearInterval(i); else step(); }, 200); }
  draw();
</script>
:::

::: layer L4 deeper
For production systems, there is also speculative decoding: a small draft model (e.g., 1B parameters) generates 4–8 candidate tokens, and the large model (70B+) verifies them all in a single forward pass. This can 2–3× speed up inference without changing the output distribution. The draft model is a separate neural network running in parallel.
:::

::: quiz mcq L4
In autoregressive generation, why is the KV-cache necessary?
- [ ] It stores the model's weights
- [ ] It compresses the vocabulary
- [x] It avoids recomputing attention for previously generated tokens
- [ ] It trains the model during inference
explain: The KV-cache stores key and value tensors from previous tokens so each new token only needs to attend to the new state, not recompute all previous attention.
:::

::: grounding
includes: The complete inference pipeline from user prompt to generated response including all external infrastructure
know: LLM inference is an autoregressive loop where each generated token becomes input for the next generation step [vaswani2017]
know: The KV-cache stores past key and value tensors to avoid recomputing attention for previous tokens [pope2022]
know: Temperature, top-k, and top-p sampling are post-processing steps applied to logits outside the neural network [holtzman2020]
source: vaswani2017 | Attention Is All You Need | https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762
source: pope2022 | Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference | https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.05102
source: holtzman2020 | The Curious Case of Neural Text Degeneration | https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.09751
:::
